The '''2011 FA Cup final''' was the 130th [[FA Cup Final|final]] of the [[FA Cup]], the world's oldest domestic [[Association football|football]] cup competition.<ref>{{cite news |title=History of The FA Cup |url=http://www.thefa.com/Competitions/FACompetitions/TheFACup/History/HistoryOfTheFACup.aspx |work=TheFA.com |publisher=The Football Association |access-date=10 December 2011 |quote=TheFA.com takes a brief look at the rich history of the oldest and best domestic Cup competition in the world. }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=FA Cup first round draw details |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/15481886 |publisher=BBC Sport |date=30 October 2011 |access-date=10 December 2011 |quote=The FA Cup is the world's oldest association football club competition}}</ref> The final took place on 14 May 2011 at [[Wembley Stadium]] in London in front of 88,643 spectators and a British television audience of more than eight million.<ref name="viewing">{{cite news |first=John |last=Plunkett |title=FA Cup final nets 8m viewers |url=https://www.theguardian.com/media/2011/may/16/fa-cup-final-8m-viewers-itv1 |work=The Guardian |date=16 May 2011 |access-date=18 May 2011 }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=FA seeking to avoid Cup final clash with Premier League |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/fa_cup/8956524.stm |publisher=BBC Sport |date=31 August 2010 |access-date=4 March 2011 }}</ref> The clubs contesting the final were [[Premier League]] clubs [[Manchester City F.C.|Manchester City]] and [[Stoke City F.C.|Stoke City]]. The match was Stoke City's first FA Cup final, and Manchester City's ninth.

As Premier League clubs, they entered the competition in the [[2010–11 FA Cup#Third round proper|third round]]. Manchester City made an unconvincing start, contesting two [[Replay (sports)|replays]] against lower league opposition in the third and fourth rounds but gained momentum and kept three consecutive [[clean sheets]] en route to the final. Stoke City played one replay in the third round, before beating all opponents in a run which culminated in a 5–0 victory over [[Bolton Wanderers F.C.|Bolton Wanderers]] in the semi-final at Wembley Stadium – the biggest winning margin at Wembley since 1939.<ref>{{cite news |first=John |last=Ley |title=Bolton Wanderers 0 Stoke City 5: match report |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/competitions/fa-cup/8450820/Bolton-Wanderers-0-Stoke-City-5-match-report.html |work=[[The Daily Telegraph]]|location=London |date=17 April 2011 |access-date=18 December 2011 }}</ref> Manchester City entered the final as favourites,<ref>{{cite news |title=Stoke's Whelan not seeking Man City revenge in Cup |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/teams/s/stoke_city/9480355.stm |publisher=BBC Sport |date=12 May 2011 |access-date=18 December 2011 }}</ref> with Stoke City as [[Underdog (competition)|underdogs]].<ref name="underdog">{{cite news |first=Phil |last=Shaw |title=We are biggest underdogs since the Crazy Gang, claims Pulis |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/fa-league-cups/we-are-biggest-underdogs-since-the-crazy-gang-claims-pulis-2282546.html |work=[[The Independent]] |publisher=Independent Print |date=12 May 2011 |access-date=17 May 2011 |archive-date=14 May 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110514112832/http://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/fa-league-cups/we-are-biggest-underdogs-since-the-crazy-gang-claims-pulis-2282546.html |url-status=dead }}</ref>

Manchester City began the match the brighter of the two teams with the majority of possession and a number of [[Shooting (association football)|shots]] forcing saves from goalkeeper [[Thomas Sørensen]] but the first half remained goalless. Stoke improved after the [[half-time]] interval but failed to score from their only shot on target in the 62nd minute, which was saved by goalkeeper [[Joe Hart]] after a one-on-one with striker [[Kenwyne Jones]]. In the 74th minute, Manchester City midfielder, [[Yaya Touré]] fired a loose ball in the Stoke City penalty area past goalkeeper Sørensen to give Manchester City the lead. Stoke attempted to equalise after Manchester City's goal without success and the final finished 1–0 with Manchester City claiming their fifth FA Cup.<ref>{{cite news |title=City end 35 years of hurt by winning FA Cup |url=http://soccernet.espn.go.com/report/_/id/316112?cc=5739 |work=[[ESPN Soccernet]] |publisher=ESPN Internet Ventures |date=14 May 2011 |access-date=16 May 2011 |archive-date=18 May 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110518043842/http://soccernet.espn.go.com/report/_/id/316112?cc=5739 |url-status=dead }}</ref> The result gave Manchester City their first major trophy for 35 years, ending the longest trophy drought in the club's history.<ref name="bbcmcnulty"/><ref>{{cite news |first=Duncan |last=White |title=FA Cup final: Manchester City 1 Stoke City 0 match report |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/competitions/fa-cup/8514022/FA-Cup-final-Manchester-City-1-Stoke-City-0-match-report.html |work=The Daily Telegraph|location=London |date=16 May 2011 |access-date=16 May 2011 }}</ref> Stoke City manager [[Tony Pulis]] said "Manchester City were the better team and deserved to win",<ref name="proud">{{cite news |title=Stoke boss Tony Pulis proud despite FA Cup defeat |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/13400966 |publisher=BBC Sport |date=14 May 2011 |access-date=16 May 2011 }}</ref> but expressed "disappointment" at his team's display.<ref name="proud"/> Manchester City manager [[Roberto Mancini]] dedicated the victory to the [[Manchester City F.C. supporters|Manchester City supporters]], declaring, "I am happy for the fans, they deserved to win this Cup. For a long time they didn't win."<ref name="dedicate">{{cite news |title=Roberto Mancini dedicates FA Cup win to Man City fans |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/13401678 |publisher=BBC Sport |date=14 May 2011 |access-date=17 May 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110515111641/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/13401678.stm |archive-date=15 May 2011 |url-status=live }}</ref>

The medals were handed out by [[Prime Minister of the United Kingdom|Prime Minister]] [[David Cameron]]. As winners, Manchester City won a place in the [[2011 FA Community Shield]] and the [[2011–12 UEFA Europa League]] but as they had already qualified for the [[UEFA Champions League]] via their league position, the Europa League place was given to Stoke City as runners-up. To celebrate their victory, Manchester City held an open-top bus parade on 23 May 2011, beginning at [[Manchester Town Hall]] and ending at the [[City of Manchester Stadium]]; it attracted a crowd of up to 100,000.<ref>{{cite news |first=Chris |last=Nield |title=FA Cup parade: Your photos |url=http://www.mcfc.co.uk/news/club-news/2011/may/fa-cup-homecoming-fan-photos |work=mcfc.co.uk |publisher=Manchester City FC |date=24 May 2011 |access-date=23 December 2011 |archive-date=17 December 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20111217090739/http://www.mcfc.co.uk/News/Club-news/2011/May/FA-Cup-homecoming-fan-photos |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |title=Manchester United fans brave rain for parade |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/premier-league/manchester-united-fans-brave-rain-for-parade-2290963.html |work=The Independent |publisher=Independent Print Limited |date=30 May 2011 |access-date=23 December 2011 |archive-date=20 August 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110820022413/http://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/premier-league/manchester-united-fans-brave-rain-for-parade-2290963.html |url-status=dead }}</ref>

==Route to the final==
{{see also|2010–11 FA Cup}}

===Manchester City===
[[File:Wembley Manchester derby after final whistle.JPG|thumb|left|After the semi-final against [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]], which Manchester City won 1–0 to secure a place in the 2011 final.|alt=A full football stadium, with spectators in the left half wearing blue, and those in the right half wearing red]]
As a [[Premier League]] team, Manchester City entered the competition in the third round. Their opening match was an away draw at [[Leicester City F.C.|Leicester City]]. Following a supporter campaign, Manchester City dedicated the match to former striker [[Neil Young (footballer born 1944)|Neil Young]], who was terminally ill.<ref>{{cite news |title=Red and black scarf reminder |url=http://www.mcfc.co.uk/News/Club-news/2010/December/Red-and-black-for-Leicester |work=mcfc.co.uk |publisher=Manchester City FC |date=31 December 2010 |access-date=4 January 2011 |archive-date=8 March 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160308095104/http://www.mcfc.co.uk/news/club-news/2010/december/red-and-black-for-leicester |url-status=dead }}</ref> Young scored the winning goal when Manchester City and Leicester City met in the [[1969 FA Cup Final]].<ref>{{cite news |first=Mark |last=Odgen |title=Roberto Mancini to lead red-and-black scarf tribute to Manchester City FA Cup hero Neil Young |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/teams/manchester-city/8246770/Roberto-Mancini-to-lead-red-and-black-scarf-tribute-to-Manchester-City-FA-Cup-hero-Neil-Young.html |work=The Daily Telegraph|location=London |date=7 January 2011 |access-date=9 January 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110120191850/http://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/teams/manchester-city/8246770/Roberto-Mancini-to-lead-red-and-black-scarf-tribute-to-Manchester-City-FA-Cup-hero-Neil-Young.html |archive-date=20 January 2011 |url-status=live }}</ref> Manchester City fell behind after 46 seconds when [[Sol Bamba]] scored for Leicester following a corner. Manchester City took the lead by half-time through [[James Milner]] and [[Carlos Tevez]], but [[Andy King (footballer born 1988)|Andy King]] equalised midway through the second half to make the score 2–2.<ref>{{cite news |first=Stuart |last=James |title=Sol Bamba proves a handful to earn Leicester replay at Manchester City |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2011/jan/09/leicester-city-manchester-city-fa-cup-third-round |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=9 January 2011 |access-date=17 April 2011 }}</ref> The tie was replayed at the [[City of Manchester Stadium]] the following week. Tevez gave Manchester City the lead after quarter of an hour, but the lead was brief. Four minutes later, [[Paul Gallagher (footballer)|Paul Gallagher]]'s penalty levelled the score, after [[Patrick Vieira]] had fouled [[Lloyd Dyer]].<ref>{{cite news |first=Sonia |last=Oxley |title=Man City sink gallant Leicester in FA Cup replay |url=http://uk.reuters.com/article/uk-soccer-england-cup-idUKTRE70H79120110118 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160306151814/http://uk.reuters.com/article/uk-soccer-england-cup-idUKTRE70H79120110118 |url-status=dead |archive-date=6 March 2016 |work=[[Reuters]] |publisher=[[Thomson Reuters]] |date=18 January 2011 |access-date=16 July 2011 }}</ref> Before half-time, two Manchester City goals in 90 seconds shifted the momentum of the match. In the second half, Tevez missed a penalty and a goal by Dyer made the score 3–2. Leicester then pushed for an equaliser, but instead [[Aleksandar Kolarov]] scored on a counter-attack to make the final score 4–2.<ref>{{cite news |first=Phil |last=Dawkes |title=Man City 4–2 Leicester |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/fa_cup/9363877.stm |publisher=BBC Sport |date=18 January 2011 |access-date=16 July 2011 }}</ref>

