For the fourth round Manchester City were drawn against [[Football League One|League One]] [[Notts County F.C.|Notts County]] at [[Meadow Lane]]. On a pitch described by the BBC as "pudding-like", lower division County threatened an upset when [[Neal Bishop]] scored from a corner in the 59th minute.<ref>{{cite news |first=James |last=Lillywhite |title=Notts County 1–1 Man City |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/fa_cup/9379084.stm |publisher=BBC Sport |date=30 January 2011 |access-date=18 May 2011 }}</ref> However, ten minutes from time [[Micah Richards]] crossed for [[Edin Džeko]] to score his first Manchester City goal, ensuring a replay at the City of Manchester Stadium.<ref>{{cite news |first=Henry |last=Winter |title=Notts County 1 Manchester City 1: match report |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/competitions/fa-cup/8291438/Notts-County-1-Manchester-City-1-match-report.html |work=The Daily Telegraph|location=London |date=30 January 2011 |access-date=30 July 2011 }}</ref> Notts County started the rematch brightly, but faded as the game progressed. The score remained 0–0 for most of the first half, but Vieira scored either side of half-time to give Manchester City a two-goal lead.<ref>{{cite news |first=Chris |last=Whyatt |title=Man City 5–0 Notts County |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/fa_cup/9401152.stm |publisher=BBC Sport |date=21 February 2011 |access-date=2 August 2011 }}</ref> From that point, the match proved less even, and Manchester City scored three more goals for a 5–0 win.<ref>{{cite news |first=Rory |last=Smith |title=Manchester City 5 Notts County 0: match report |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/competitions/fa-cup/8332402/Manchester-City-5-Notts-County-0-match-report.html |work=The Daily Telegraph|location=London |date=20 February 2011 |access-date=2 August 2011 }}</ref> From there on, City kept consecutive clean sheets on the way to winning the cup. Manchester City's fifth-round match was against [[Aston Villa F.C.|Aston Villa]] at the City of Manchester Stadium. Villa manager [[Gérard Houllier]] rested several senior players, in contrast to a near full-strength Manchester City line-up.<ref>{{cite news |first=Daniel |last=Taylor |title=Manchester City's Yaya Touré makes Aston Villa pay for key omissions |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2011/mar/02/manchester-city-aston-villa-fa-cup |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=2 March 2011 |access-date=2 August 2011 }}</ref> Manchester City took the lead after less than five minutes through Yaya Touré, and further goals from [[Mario Balotelli]] and [[David Silva]] resulted in a comfortable 3–0 win.<ref>{{cite news |first=Dan |last=Quarrell |title=FA Cup – City sweep past under-strength Villa |url=http://uk.eurosport.yahoo.com/02032011/58/fa-cup-city-sweep-past-under-strength-villa.html |work=[[Eurosport]] |publisher=Yahoo! |date=2 March 2011 |access-date=2 August 2011 }}</ref>

In the sixth round, Manchester City were again at home, and faced [[Reading F.C.|Reading]], the only non-Premier League team left in the competition.<ref>{{cite news |first=Daniel |last=Taylor |title=Manchester City head for United showdown after disposing of Reading |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2011/mar/13/manchester-city-reading-fa-cup1 |work=The Guardian |date=13 March 2011 |access-date=2 August 2011 }}</ref> Micah Richards scored the only goal, a header from a corner in the 73rd minute.<ref>{{cite news |first=Simon |last=Stone |title=Man City set up United FA Cup showdown |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/fa-league-cups/man-city-set-up-united-fa-cup-showdown-2240887.html |work=independent.co.uk |publisher=Independent Print |date=13 March 2011 |access-date=2 August 2011 |archive-date=16 March 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110316170205/http://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/fa-league-cups/man-city-set-up-united-fa-cup-showdown-2240887.html |url-status=dead }}</ref> In the semi-final, at [[Wembley Stadium]], City defeated rivals [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]] 1–0 with a Yaya Touré goal,<ref>{{cite news |first=Duncan |last=White |title=Manchester City 1 Manchester United 0: match report |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/competitions/fa-cup/8450791/Manchester-City-1-Manchester-United-0-match-report.html |work=The Daily Telegraph|location=London |date=16 April 2011 |access-date=21 May 2011 }}</ref> and continued their [[FA Cup Semi-finals#Semi-finals table|FA Cup semi-final record]] of nine victories out of 11, reaching the FA Cup Final for the first time since [[1981 FA Cup final|1981]].

