Stoke, also a Premier League side, entered the competition at the third round stage, where they were drawn at home to Welsh side [[Cardiff City F.C.|Cardiff City]]. Stoke City made seven changes to the team that played their previous match.<ref>{{cite news |first=Joe |last=Lovejoy |title=Cardiff win replay with Stoke City for match neither manager wanted |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2011/jan/08/stoke-city-cardiff-city-fa-cup |work=The Guardian |date=8 January 2011 |access-date=1 June 2011 }}</ref> [[Michael Chopra]] gave Cardiff an early lead, but Stoke's [[Tuncay Şanlı]] levelled the score just before half time. Stoke had a number of chances in the later stages of the match, but Cardiff held out for a replay.<ref>{{cite news |title=Stoke 1–1 Cardiff |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/fa_cup/9341983.stm |publisher=BBC Sport |date=8 January 2011 |access-date=1 June 2011 }}</ref> In the replay at the [[Cardiff City Stadium]] Stoke again used several reserves, changing all but one of the players from the previous league match.<ref name="telegraphstoker3">{{cite news |first=Graham |last=Clutton |title=Cardiff City 0 Stoke City 2 (aet): match report |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/competitions/fa-cup/8267151/Cardiff-City-0-Stoke-City-2-aet-match-report.html |work=The Daily Telegraph|location=London |date=18 January 2011 |access-date=1 June 2011 }}</ref> Few chances occurred during the game; the score was 0–0 after 90 minutes, prompting [[extra time]].<ref name="guardianstoker3">{{cite news |first=Joe |last=Lovejoy |title=Jon Walters runs through Cardiff as Stoke win in extra time |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2011/jan/18/cardiff-city-stoke-city-fa-cup |work=The Guardian |date=18 January 2011 |access-date=1 June 2011 }}</ref> In the added period, [[Jonathan Walters]] scored twice to secure a 2–0 Stoke City win; the first goal was a header from a [[corner kick|corner]],<ref name="guardianstoker3"/> the second a near-post rebound.<ref name="telegraphstoker3"/> Stoke City then travelled to fellow Midlands team [[Wolverhampton Wanderers F.C.|Wolverhampton Wanderers]] for the fourth round, in one of five all-Premier League ties.<ref>{{cite news |title=The fourth round Draw |url=http://nav.thefa.com/sitecore/content/TheFA/Home/TheFACup/FACompetitions/TheFACup/NewsAndFeatures/2011/4th-round-draw |work=TheFA.com |publisher=The Football Association |date=9 January 2011 |access-date=6 July 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120324030848/http://nav.thefa.com/sitecore/content/TheFA/Home/TheFACup/FACompetitions/TheFACup/NewsAndFeatures/2011/4th-round-draw |archive-date=24 March 2012 |url-status=dead }}</ref> Stoke defender [[Robert Huth]] was a central figure in the match. Ten minutes from time he gave Stoke a 1–0 lead with a header from a free-kick,<ref>{{cite news |first=John |last=Curtis |title=Huth earns Stoke FA Cup victory over Wolves |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/fa-league-cups/huth-earns-stoke-fa-cup-victory-over-wolves-2198827.html |work=independent.co.uk |publisher=Independent Print |date=30 January 2011 |access-date=7 July 2011 |archive-date=12 February 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110212074830/http://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/fa-league-cups/huth-earns-stoke-fa-cup-victory-over-wolves-2198827.html |url-status=dead }}</ref> but in the final minute he conceded a penalty for tripping [[Nenad Milijaš]]. Milijaš took the spot-kick himself, but his effort was saved by Thomas Sørensen.<ref>{{cite news |first=Stuart |last=James |title=Robert Huth header gives Stoke the edge over Wolves |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2011/jan/30/wolverhampton-wanderers-stoke-city-fa-cup |work=The Guardian |date=30 January 2011 |access-date=7 July 2011 }}</ref>

Stoke's fifth-round match was at home against [[Football League One|League One]] leaders [[Brighton & Hove Albion F.C.|Brighton & Hove Albion]]. Three first half goals by [[John Carew]], [[Jonathan Walters]], [[Ryan Shawcross]] led Stoke to a 3–0 win.<ref>{{cite news |first=Saj |last=Chowdhury |title=Stoke 3–0 Brighton |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/fa_cup/9401077.stm |publisher=BBC Sport |date=19 February 2011 |access-date=7 July 2011 }}</ref> Stoke then faced [[West Ham United F.C.|West Ham United]] in the sixth round. Stoke scored an early goal from a set-piece, when long [[throw-in]] specialist [[Rory Delap]] delivered the ball into the penalty area for Huth to score with a header.<ref>{{cite news |first=Alex |last=Chick |title=FA Cup – Muscular Stoke hold off West Ham |url=http://uk.eurosport.yahoo.com/13032011/58/fa-cup-muscular-stoke-hold-west-ham.html |work=[[Eurosport]] |publisher=Yahoo! |date=13 March 2011 |access-date=14 July 2011 }}</ref> Controversial refereeing decisions then provoked the ire of both teams. When [[Frédéric Piquionne]] equalised for West Ham, the decision to award the goal infuriated Stoke; in controlling the ball Piquionne committed what Stoke manager Tony Pulis called a "stonewall" handball.<ref>{{cite news |first=Stuart |last=James |title=Danny Higginbotham advances Stoke with a goal to beat West Ham |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2011/mar/13/stoke-city-west-ham-united-fa-cup |work=The Guardian |date=13 March 2011 |access-date=14 July 2011 }}</ref> West Ham themselves felt aggrieved in the first minute of the second half when Stoke were awarded a penalty. [[Matthew Etherington]] was adjudged to have been fouled by [[Scott Parker]] though, as the BBC correspondent wrote, "there appeared to be minimal contact".<ref name="bbcwham">{{cite news |first=Chris |last=Bevan |title=Stoke 2–1 West Ham |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/fa_cup/9421244.stm |publisher=BBC Sport |date=13 March 2011 |access-date=14 July 2011 }}</ref> [[Robert Green]] saved Etherington's penalty to keep the score level. The winning goal also came from a set piece. [[Danny Higginbotham]]'s free-kick was reached by Green but could not be stopped, and Stoke won 2–1 to reach the semi-finals for the first time since 1972.<ref name="bbcwham"/> In the semi-final, Stoke beat [[Bolton Wanderers F.C.|Bolton Wanderers]] comfortably, winning 5–0. Stoke took a three-goal lead in less than half an hour after goals from Etherington, Huth and [[Kenwyne Jones]]. Walters scored twice in the second half to complete the win. The margin of victory was the biggest in an FA Cup semi-final since 1939,<ref>{{cite news |first=Simon |last=Stone |title=Rampant Stoke demolish Bolton in FA Cup |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/fa-league-cups/rampant-stoke-demolish-bolton-in-fa-cup-2269176.html |work=The Independent |publisher=Independent Print |date=17 April 2011 |access-date=1 June 2011 |archive-date=20 April 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110420132635/http://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/fa-league-cups/rampant-stoke-demolish-bolton-in-fa-cup-2269176.html |url-status=dead }}</ref> and secured Stoke's first ever FA Cup Final appearance.<ref>{{cite news|title=Rampant City Make History |url=http://www.stokecityfc.com/page/MatchReport/0,,10310~55033,00.html |work=stokecityfc.com |publisher=Stoke City FC |date=17 April 2011 |access-date=18 April 2011 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110421073119/http://www.stokecityfc.com/page/MatchReport/0%2C%2C10310~55033%2C00.html |archive-date=21 April 2011 }}</ref> By reaching the final, Stoke qualified for the [[2011–12 UEFA Europa League]] before the final, as Manchester City had beat Tottenham days before the final to secure fourth position in the Premier League and consequently Champions League qualification.<ref>{{cite news|title=Manager Hails Europa League Boost |url=http://www.stokecityfc.com/page/NewsDetail/0,,10310~2357635,00.html? |work=stokecityfc.com |publisher=Stoke City FC |date=11 May 2011 |access-date=22 December 2011 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110717102958/http://www.stokecityfc.com/page/NewsDetail/0%2C%2C10310~2357635%2C00.html |archive-date=17 July 2011 }}</ref>

==Pre-match==
[[File:2011FACupFinalAbideWithMe.JPG|thumb|The pre-match rendition of ''[[Abide with Me]]''.]]
Manchester City were appearing in the final for the ninth time. They had won the cup four times previously (in [[1904 FA Cup final|1904]], [[1934 FA Cup final|1934]], [[1956 FA Cup final|1956]] and [[1969 FA Cup final|1969]]), and had been beaten in the final four times (in [[1926 FA Cup final|1926]], [[1933 FA Cup final|1933]], [[1955 FA Cup final|1955]] and [[1981 FA Cup final|1981]]). Stoke City were making their first appearance in the final. Their previous best was participation in the semi-finals, which had occurred three times.<ref name="Stokesemis">{{cite news |first=Carl |last=Markham |title=Stoke book place in FA Cup semi-final |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/fa-league-cups/stoke-book-place-in-fa-cup-semifinal-2240831.html |work=independent.co.uk |publisher=Independent Print |date=13 March 2011 |access-date=13 December 2011 |archive-date=16 March 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110316152211/http://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/fa-league-cups/stoke-book-place-in-fa-cup-semifinal-2240831.html |url-status=dead }}</ref>

The 2011 final faced scheduling problems and the match clashed with top flight [[Premier League|League]] fixtures for the first time since 1934, which disappointed football traditionalists.<ref name="third_rate">{{cite news |first=Rob |last=Stewart |title=FA Cup final 2011: FA accused of turning premier cup competition into 'third-rate tournament' |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/competitions/fa-cup/8503362/FA-Cup-final-2011-FA-accused-of-turning-premier-cup-competition-into-third-rate-tournament.html |work=The Daily Telegraph|location=London |date=9 May 2011 |access-date=19 May 2011 }}</ref> The [[2011 UEFA Champions League Final]] was being hosted at Wembley on 28 May 2011 and UEFA rules stipulate that the host stadium for the final must not have a fixture played two weeks prior to the final. Therefore, the FA Cup final had to be moved forward, and for the first time since 1989 was played before the [[Football in England|English football]] domestic season ended.

[[File:2011FACupFinalTeams.JPG|thumb|left|The teams line up before the match with Stoke City in red-and-white stripes and Manchester City in sky blue.]]
Many of the weekend's Premier League fixtures were rescheduled to avoid a clash with the final, with some kicking off at 12:45 on Saturday and others on Sunday.<ref name="third_rate"/><ref>{{cite news |first=Ben |last=Rumsby |title=Fixtures moved to avoid clash with FA Cup final |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/news-and-comment/fixtures-moved-to-avoid-clash-with-fa-cup-final-2255520.html |work=independent.co.uk |publisher=Independent Print |date=28 March 2011 |access-date=18 May 2011 |archive-date=12 November 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121112013448/http://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/news-and-comment/fixtures-moved-to-avoid-clash-with-fa-cup-final-2255520.html |url-status=dead }}</ref> By coincidence, Manchester City and Stoke City were scheduled to meet in the league on cup final day. The league match was rearranged for the Wednesday after the final. This prompted criticism from [[Tottenham Hotspur F.C.|Tottenham Hotspur]] manager [[Harry Redknapp]], whose team was challenging Manchester City for a place in the 2011–12 Champions League. Redknapp stated that if Manchester City won the final, it would then be in Stoke City's interest to lose against Manchester City in the league match, as it would make [[UEFA Europa League|Europa League]] qualification for Stoke more likely.<ref>{{cite news |title=Harry Redknapp wants Man City-Stoke match date change |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/13130370 |publisher=BBC Sport |date=19 April 2011 |access-date=21 May 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110420044351/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/13130370.stm |archive-date=20 April 2011 |url-status=live }}</ref> Stoke City refuted this accusation strongly,<ref>{{cite news |first=Stuart |last=James |title=Stoke City chairman backs date for Manchester City league match |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2011/apr/20/stoke-manchester-city-premier-league-tottenham |work=The Guardian |date=20 April 2011 |access-date=18 December 2011 }}</ref> but ultimately this scenario was avoided. Champions League qualification was settled four days before the final, when Manchester City beat Tottenham Hotspur 1–0 in a league match.<ref>{{cite news |first=Phil |last=McNulty |title=Man City 1–0 Tottenham |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/eng_prem/9480251.stm |publisher=BBC Sport |date=10 May 2011 |access-date=27 November 2011 }}</ref>

Both clubs received an allocation of approximately 25,000 tickets,<ref name="tickets">{{cite news |title=FA Cup final ticket prices increased by up to 22% |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/13081660 |publisher=BBC Sport |date=14 April 2011 |access-date=17 May 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110422083307/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/13081660.stm |archive-date=22 April 2011 |url-status=live }}</ref> fewer than the 32,000 received for the semi-finals. The allocation was enough for the 21,000 Stoke City season ticket holders, but not for Manchester City's 36,000 season ticket holders.<ref>{{cite news |first=Mike |last=Keegan |title=11,000 Manchester City season ticket holders set to miss out on Wembley FA Cup Final tickets |url=http://menmedia.co.uk/manchestereveningnews/news/s/1418617_11000-manchester-city-season-ticket-holders-set-to-miss-out-on-wembley-fa-cup-final-tickets |work=[[Manchester Evening News]] |publisher=MEN Media |date=21 April 2011 |access-date=17 May 2011 |archive-date=12 November 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121112182328/http://menmedia.co.uk/manchestereveningnews/news/s/1418617_11000-manchester-city-season-ticket-holders-set-to-miss-out-on-wembley-fa-cup-final-tickets |url-status=dead }}</ref> Consequently, some supporters felt aggrieved at the lack of tickets,<ref>{{cite news |title=Comment: Tickets, tension and the FA 'family' |url=http://menmedia.co.uk/manchestereveningnews/comment/blogs/s/1418623_comment-tickets-tension-and-the-fa-family |work=Manchester Evening News |publisher=MEN Media |date=21 April 2011 |access-date=17 May 2011 |archive-date=26 April 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110426175234/http://menmedia.co.uk/manchestereveningnews/comment/blogs/s/1418623_comment-tickets-tension-and-the-fa-family |url-status=dead }}</ref> including Stoke City chairman [[Peter Coates]] who expressed his disappointment and suggested an allocation in the region of 30,000 for both clubs.<ref>{{cite news |first=Martin |last=Spinks |title=Stoke City: Coates calls for increased allocations for FA Cup final |url=http://www.thisisstaffordshire.co.uk/news/Stoke-City-Coates-calls-increased-allocations-FA-Cup-final/article-3468320-detail/article.html |archive-url=https://archive.today/20130505071326/http://www.thisisstaffordshire.co.uk/news/Stoke-City-Coates-calls-increased-allocations-FA-Cup-final/article-3468320-detail/article.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=5 May 2013 |work=The Sentinel |publisher=Staffordshire Sentinel News and Media |date=20 April 2011 |access-date=18 May 2011 }}</ref> Ticket prices for the final exceeded £100 for the first time. The most expensive tickets cost £115, an increase of 22&nbsp;per&nbsp;cent on the previous season. The cheapest tickets available at £45, up £5 from 2010.<ref name="tickets"/><ref>{{cite news |last1=Pitt-Brooke |first1=Jack |title=Chelsea raise Stamford Bridge ticket price to £87 |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/premier-league/chelsea-raise-stamford-bridge-ticket-price-to-16387-2267923.html |access-date=3 September 2020 |work=The Guardian |date=15 April 2011 |archive-date=29 December 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121229041506/http://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/premier-league/chelsea-raise-stamford-bridge-ticket-price-to-16387-2267923.html |url-status=dead }}</ref>

As for every match in the 2010–11 FA Cup, the ball for the final was provided by [[Nike, Inc.|Nike]]-owned, [[Manchester]]-based sports equipment company [[Umbro]]. The Umbro Neo Pro features a 14-panel design, and is patterned in blue and red.<ref name="finalball">{{cite news |title=Cup Final balls arrive at Wembley |url=http://nav.thefa.com/sitecore/content/TheFA/Home/TheFACup/FACompetitions/TheFACup/Match-Centres-2010-11/Final/Manchester-City-v-Stoke-City/Match-Previews/FA-Cup-Final-match-ball |work=TheFA.com |publisher=The Football Association |date=11 May 2011 |access-date=10 February 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120820044615/http://nav.thefa.com/sitecore/content/TheFA/Home/TheFACup/FACompetitions/TheFACup/Match-Centres-2010-11/Final/Manchester-City-v-Stoke-City/Match-Previews/FA-Cup-Final-match-ball |archive-date=20 August 2012 |url-status=dead }}</ref> The majority of the balls to be used in the game arrived on the previous Wednesday, while the ball to be used at kick-off was delivered to the stadium on the day of the game.<ref name="finalball"/>

The traditional Cup Final hymn, "[[Abide with Me]]", was sung by Tenors Unlimited, a trio of male vocalists, and former ''[[The X Factor (UK)|X Factor]]'' contestant [[Stacey Solomon]] performed the national anthem, "[[God Save the Queen]]".<ref>{{cite news |title=Solomon to perform at Wembley |url=http://www.thefa.com/Competitions/FACompetitions/TheFACup/Match-Centres-2010-11/Final/Manchester-City-v-Stoke-City/Match-Previews/Stacey-Solomon-perform-FA-Cup-anthem.aspx |work=TheFA.com |publisher=The Football Association |date=18 April 2011 |access-date=10 February 2012}}</ref>
