Stoke City went into their first FA Cup Final as [[Underdog (competition)|underdogs]] according to their manager [[Tony Pulis]],<ref name="underdog"/> while Manchester City manager [[Roberto Mancini]] saw in-form Stoke's underdog tag as a risk for complacency from his side.<ref>{{cite news |title=Roberto Mancini wary of Stoke FA Cup final threat |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/13382387 |publisher=BBC Sport |date=13 May 2011 |access-date=17 May 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110514111848/http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/13382387.stm |archive-date=14 May 2011 |url-status=live }}</ref> There were fitness doubts over key players for both teams, [[Carlos Tevez]] for Manchester City,<ref>{{cite news |first=Simon |last=Stone |title=Carlos Tevez losing FA Cup final fitness battle |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/premier-league/carlos-tevez-losing-fa-cup-final-fitness-battle-2279343.html |work=independent.co.uk |publisher=Independent Print |date=5 May 2011 |access-date=19 May 2011 |archive-date=6 May 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110506040859/http://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football/premier-league/carlos-tevez-losing-fa-cup-final-fitness-battle-2279343.html |url-status=dead }}</ref> and [[Matthew Etherington]] for Stoke City, who suffered a hamstring tear only 17 days prior to the final,<ref>{{cite news |title=Stoke's Matthew Etherington has scan on hamstring injury as he faces final countdown to be fit for FA Cup final |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/teams/stoke-city/8480802/Stokes-Matthew-Etherington-has-scan-on-hamstring-injury-as-he-faces-final-countdown-to-be-fit-for-FA-Cup-final.html |work=The Daily Telegraph|location=London |date=28 April 2011 |access-date=19 May 2011 }}</ref> but both were able to start the match. However, Stoke City's underdog tag proved true as Mancini's Manchester City side had the better of the match, with a 59&nbsp;per&nbsp;cent share of ball possession and 23 attempts on goal to Stoke City's nine, only one of which was on target.

Both teams were able to play in their respective home colours without any colour clash: Stoke City played in their red-and-white stripes, and Manchester City played in their sky blue colours with the [[Manchester City Council#Coat of arms|Manchester coat of arms]] on their shirt numbers, keeping up a long-standing club tradition of wearing the city's coat of arms for cup finals.<ref>{{Cite news |author=Aaron |title=City inspired by the past ahead of the FA Cup Final |url=http://blog.umbro.com/post/city-inspired-by-the-past-ahead-of-the-fa-cup-final/ |work=blog.umbro.com |publisher=Umbro |date=12 May 2011 |access-date=23 December 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120404190714/http://blog.umbro.com/post/city-inspired-by-the-past-ahead-of-the-fa-cup-final/ |archive-date=4 April 2012 |url-status=dead }}</ref>

===Report===
Manchester City set up in a [[Formation (association football)#4–2–3–1|4–2–3–1 formation]],<ref name="balotelli">{{cite news |first=Jeremy |last=Wilson |title=Manchester City striker Mario Balotelli shows maturity in FA Cup final as he gives man-of-the-match display |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/8514470/Manchester-City-striker-Mario-Balotelli-shows-maturity-in-FA-Cup-final-as-he-gives-man-of-the-match-display.html |work=The Daily Telegraph|location=London |date=14 May 2011 |access-date=29 August 2011 }}</ref> with [[Mario Balotelli]], [[David Silva]] and [[Yaya Touré]] playing behind lone striker Carlos Tevez, and [[Nigel de Jong]] and [[Gareth Barry]] in defensive midfield roles.<ref name="lineup">{{cite news |title=Manchester City vs Stoke City – Team line-up & ratings |url=http://uk.eurosport.yahoo.com/football/fa-cup/2010-2011/manchester-city-stoke-city-429060.html |work=Eurosport |publisher=Yahoo! |date=14 May 2011 |access-date=22 December 2011 }}</ref> Stoke City set up in a rigid [[Formation (association football)#4–4–2|4–4–2 formation]], with two wingers in [[Jermaine Pennant]] and Matthew Etherington<ref>{{cite news |first=David |last=Pleat |title=Manchester City must counter Stoke's wingers as well as long throws |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/blog/2011/may/13/manchester-city-stoke-city-tactics |work=The Guardian |date=13 May 2011 |access-date=22 December 2011 }}</ref> to support strikers [[Kenwyne Jones]] and [[Jonathan Walters]].<ref name="lineup"/>

Manchester City started sprightly, managing to create some chances which forced saves from Stoke goalkeeper [[Thomas Sørensen]] and steady defending to keep Stoke City in the game in the first half. Yaya Touré unleashed a 30-yard strike in the 11th minute which went only inches wide of the top corner of the goal,<ref name="teleasithappened">{{cite news |first=Thom |last=Gibbs |title=FA Cup final 2011: Manchester City v Stoke City live |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/competitions/fa-cup/8513729/FA-Cup-final-2011-Manchester-City-v-Stoke-City-live.html |work=The Daily Telegraph|location=London |date=14 May 2011 |access-date=28 November 2011 }}</ref> while Mario Balotelli had a curling shot from the corner of the penalty area in the 24th minute which was destined for the goal but for a strong, one-handed save from Sørensen.<ref name="teleasithappened"/>

Stoke improved after the break, but Manchester City had a key opportunity in the 56th minute when a counter-attack led by Carlos Tevez, who drifted out to the right flank, allowed David Silva to find space outside the Stoke City penalty area. Tevez managed to pass to Silva, but Silva was guilty of over-elaboration instead of shooting first-time, and the Stoke City defence managed to regroup, dispossessing Silva before he could adjust himself to shoot at goal.<ref name="teleasithappened"/>

Only six minutes later, in the 62nd minute,<ref name="teleasithappened"/> Stoke had their key chance and only shot on target of the match with Kenwyne Jones in the 61st minute. A looping ball over the top of the Manchester City defence by Matthew Etherington allowed Jones a one-on-one, but he failed to convert, firing straight at goalkeeper Joe Hart with the ball ricocheting off both striker and keeper before bobbling to safety. Doubts over Matthew Etherington's fitness resurfaced when he was taken off in the 63rd minute,<ref>{{cite news |title=Etherington firm over fitness |url=http://www.skysports.com/story/0,19528,11701_6935780,00.html |publisher=[[Sky Sports]] |date=16 May 2011 |access-date=19 May 2011 }}</ref> with Stoke City still in the game at 0–0 as it stood.

However, in the 74th minute,<ref name="teleasithappened"/> Sørensen was unable to prevent Yaya Touré's effort from ten yards when he pounced on a stray ball in the penalty area to shoot with his left foot past Sørensen in front of the Manchester City supporters. Stoke City attempted to get a goal back, resorting to direct long balls into the Manchester City penalty area but failed to create any concrete chances. Despite Yaya Touré clinching the winning goal, enigmatic striker Mario Balotelli – who claimed he had a "shit" season in a post-match interview<ref>{{cite news |first=Daniel |last=Taylor |title=Roberto Mancini targets league title after 'small piece of history' |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2011/may/14/manchester-city-stoke-city-quotes |work=The Guardian |date=14 May 2011 |access-date=26 November 2011 }}</ref> – was named man of the match for his efforts.<ref name="balotelli"/>

