==Post-match==
[[File:2011FACupFinalWinners.JPG|thumb|right|The Manchester City team celebrating winning the FA Cup]]
Tony Pulis commented after the game that "Manchester City were the better team" and that "they deserved to win the game",<ref name="proud"/> while Roberto Mancini dedicated the victory to the Manchester City supporters.<ref name="dedicate"/> Pundits [[Graham Taylor (footballer)|Graham Taylor]] and [[Mark Lawrenson]] agreed that Manchester City deserved to win but expressed disappointment at Stoke City's under-par performance in the final.<ref>{{cite news |first1=Graham |last1=Taylor |first2=Mark |last2=Lawrenson |title=BBC pundits on Man City-Stoke FA Cup final |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/fa_cup/9486202.stm |publisher=BBC Sport |date=14 May 2011 |access-date=23 December 2011}}</ref>

[[Prime Minister of the United Kingdom]], [[David Cameron]] awarded the medals to individual players at Wembley Stadium.<ref>{{cite news |title=David Cameron to attend "magical" Cup Final |url=http://nav.thefa.com/sitecore/content/TheFA/Home/TheFACup/FACompetitions/TheFACup/Match-Centres-2010-11/Final/Manchester-City-v-Stoke-City/Match-Previews/FA-Cup-Final-David-cameron-pm |work=TheFA.com |publisher=The Football Association |date=12 May 2011 |access-date=10 February 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120820044640/http://nav.thefa.com/sitecore/content/TheFA/Home/TheFACup/FACompetitions/TheFACup/Match-Centres-2010-11/Final/Manchester-City-v-Stoke-City/Match-Previews/FA-Cup-Final-David-cameron-pm |archive-date=20 August 2012 |url-status=dead }}</ref> Presenting the trophy was [[British Army]] [[Corporal#United Kingdom|Corporal]] Mark Ward, a lifelong Manchester City supporter who [[War in Afghanistan (2001–2021)|served in Afghanistan]] and whose bravery was rewarded with the [[Military Cross]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Corporal Ward is Guest of Honour |url=http://nav.thefa.com/sitecore/content/TheFA/Home/TheFACup/FACompetitions/TheFACup/Match-Centres-2010-11/Final/Manchester-City-v-Stoke-City/Match-Previews/FA-Cup-Final-chief-guest |work=TheFA.com |publisher=The Football Association |date=11 May 2011 |access-date=10 February 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120820044623/http://nav.thefa.com/sitecore/content/TheFA/Home/TheFACup/FACompetitions/TheFACup/Match-Centres-2010-11/Final/Manchester-City-v-Stoke-City/Match-Previews/FA-Cup-Final-chief-guest |archive-date=20 August 2012 |url-status=dead }}</ref> Corporal Ward presented the trophy to the winning captain, Carlos Tevez, after the game.<ref>{{cite news |title='Courageous' soldier Mark Ward to present FA Cup |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-13373884 |publisher=BBC News |date=12 May 2011 |access-date=15 May 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110513060630/http://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-13373884 |archive-date=13 May 2011 |url-status=live }}</ref>

Manchester City's victory set up a [[FA Community Shield|Community Shield]] match against rivals [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]] – who City had beaten in the semi-finals – after United clinched the Premier League shortly before kick-off in the Final. The FA Cup winners are usually awarded qualification for the [[UEFA Europa League]], but because Manchester City qualified for the [[UEFA Champions League]] via their league position, the Europa League place was passed to Stoke City as runners-up. In the [[2011 FA Community Shield]] match in August, the FA Cup winners Manchester City lost 3–2 to Manchester United after creating a 2–0 lead at half time.<ref>{{cite news |first=Chris |last=Bevan |title=Man City 2–3 Man Utd |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/14420151 |publisher=BBC Sport |date=7 August 2011 |access-date=29 August 2011 }}</ref>

Manchester United winning the [[2010–11 Premier League]] title earlier in the day meant double pride for Manchester as its teams claimed both of [[Football in England|English football]]'s [[blue ribbon]] competitions.<ref>{{cite news |title=Manchester revels in FA Cup final & Premier League joy |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/13401504 |publisher=BBC Sport |date=14 May 2011 |access-date=22 November 2011 }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |first=Barney |last=Ronay |title=Manchester mash-up pits Sky bombast against ITV sepia in a purple haze |url=https://www.theguardian.com/sport/blog/2011/may/14/manchester-sky-itv |work=The Guardian |date=14 May 2011 |access-date=10 December 2011 }}</ref> The '35 years' banner that had stood at Old Trafford, home of Manchester United which referenced to Manchester City's long trophy drought, was taken down in recognition of City's victory.<ref>{{cite news |title=Manchester United take down '35 years' banner at Old Trafford after City's FA Cup win |url=http://menmedia.co.uk/manchestereveningnews/news/s/1420898_manchester-united-take-down-35-years-banner-at-old-trafford-after-citys-fa-cup-win |work=Manchester Evening News |publisher=MEN Media |date=17 May 2011 |access-date=4 September 2011 |archive-date=9 August 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110809090945/http://menmedia.co.uk/manchestereveningnews/news/s/1420898_manchester-united-take-down-35-years-banner-at-old-trafford-after-citys-fa-cup-win |url-status=dead }}</ref>

[[File:Manchester City FC parade after winning the 2010-11 FA Cup.jpg|thumb|left|The victory parade reaches [[Portland Street, Manchester|Portland Street]].]]
The match was broadcast live in the United Kingdom by both [[ITV (TV network)|ITV]] and [[ESPN (UK)|ESPN]], with ITV providing the free-to-air coverage and ESPN being the pay-TV alternative. Television [[Broadcasters' Audience Research Board|viewing figures]] were collated after the final, with a peak of 8.5&nbsp;million viewers recorded across both channels.<ref name="viewing"/> ITV held the majority of the viewership, with a peak audience of 8.1&nbsp;million during the last 30 minutes of the final and averaged at 6.68&nbsp;million,<ref>{{cite news |first=Paul |last=Millar |title=Eurovision TV ratings reach 11-year high |url=http://www.digitalspy.co.uk/tv/news/a319564/eurovision-tv-ratings-reach-11-year-high.html |publisher=Digital Spy |date=15 May 2011 |access-date=18 May 2011 }}</ref> their highest viewing figures for an FA Cup since the competition moved to ITV from the BBC in 2009.<ref name="viewing"/> The pay-to-view alternative ESPN averaged 412,000 viewers throughout the day with a peak of 476,000.<ref>{{cite news |title=Cup Final viewing figures |url=http://www.thefa.com/Competitions/FACompetitions/TheFACup/Match-Centres-2010-11/Final/Manchester-City-v-Stoke-City/Post-Match-Reports/final-viewing-figures.aspx |publisher=The Football Association |date=16 May 2011 |access-date=17 May 2011 }}</ref>

Manchester City and Stoke City had been scheduled to play in a Premier League fixture on the day of the FA Cup Final, but instead the match had to be moved to the following Tuesday,<ref>{{cite news |first=Chris |last=Bailey |title=City move Stoke home clash |url=http://www.mcfc.co.uk/news/club-news/2011/april/stoke-game-moved |work=mcfc.co.uk |publisher=Manchester City FC |date=16 April 2011 |access-date=21 May 2011 |archive-date=23 May 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110523011105/http://www.mcfc.co.uk/News/Club-news/2011/April/Stoke-game-moved |url-status=dead }}</ref> making it the last mid-week game of the Premier League season, a match Manchester City won, 3–0.<ref>{{cite news |first=Daniel |last=Taylor |title=Carlos Tevez keeps scoring as Manchester City beat Stoke to go third |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2011/may/17/manchester-city-stoke-premier-league |work=The Guardian |date=17 May 2011 |access-date=21 May 2011 }}</ref> Despite it being Manchester City's final home game of the season, they postponed their homecoming celebrations until the post-season out of respect for the opposition.<ref>{{cite news |first=Henry |last=Winter |title=Manchester City 3 Stoke City 0: match report |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/competitions/premier-league/7944355/Manchester-City-3-Stoke-City-0-match-report.html |work=The Telegraph |date=17 May 2011 |access-date=9 November 2015 }}</ref>

Manchester City elected not to parade the trophy at the match as they did not wish to be seen as 'crass' in front of the visiting Stoke City supporters.<ref>{{cite news |title=Manchester City FA Cup victory parade to leave Albert Square at 6pm next Monday, 23 May |url=http://menmedia.co.uk/manchestereveningnews/news/s/1420799_manchester-city-fa-cup-victory-parade-to-leave-albert-square-at-6pm-next-monday-23-may |work=Manchester Evening News |publisher=MEN Media |date=16 May 2011 |access-date=17 May 2011 |archive-date=21 May 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110521141901/http://menmedia.co.uk/manchestereveningnews/news/s/1420799_manchester-city-fa-cup-victory-parade-to-leave-albert-square-at-6pm-next-monday-23-may |url-status=dead }}</ref> Instead, Manchester City were invited by [[Manchester City Council]] to host an open-top bus parade through the [[City of Manchester]] in celebration of their victory. The parade took place on 23 May 2011 and began at 18:00 starting at [[Albert Square, Manchester|Albert Square]] outside [[Manchester Town Hall]] where 10,000 people were present to see the team set off on the bus parade with various streets of the city centre closed by 12:00 in preparation for the parade.<ref>{{cite news |first=Rosie |last=Bass |title=23 May will be Blue Moon-day |url=http://www.mcfc.co.uk/news/fa-cup-countdown/2011/may/blue-moonday-may-23-victory-parade |work=mcfc.co.uk |publisher=Manchester City FC |date=14 May 2011 |access-date=10 December 2011 |archive-date=9 December 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20111209113502/http://mcfc.co.uk/News/FA-Cup-countdown/2011/May/Blue-Moonday-May-23-victory-parade |url-status=dead }}</ref> The open-top bus then travelled out of [[Manchester city centre]] via [[Princess Street, Manchester|Princess Street]], [[Portland Street, Manchester|Portland Street]], [[Piccadilly Gardens]], Newton Street on onto [[Great Ancoats Street]].<ref>{{cite news |title=Manchester City's victory FA Cup parade route revealed |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-england-manchester-13469093 |publisher=BBC News |date=20 May 2011 |access-date=29 August 2011 }}</ref> The parade finally reached Ashton New Road where thousands supporters were waiting at the gates of the City of Manchester Stadium to welcome the team home. The parade culminated in a special reception at the stadium, where 40,000 who claimed free tickets for the event were waiting.<ref>{{cite news |first=Mike |last=Keegan |title=Noel Gallagher and Badly Drawn Boy lined up for Manchester City bash |url=http://menmedia.co.uk/manchestereveningnews/news/s/1421313_noel-gallagher-and-badly-drawn-boy-lined-up-for-manchester-city-bash |work=Manchester Evening News |publisher=MEN Media |date=20 May 2011 |access-date=21 May 2011 |archive-date=24 May 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110524013126/http://menmedia.co.uk/manchestereveningnews/news/s/1421313_noel-gallagher-and-badly-drawn-boy-lined-up-for-manchester-city-bash? |url-status=dead }}</ref> [[Greater Manchester Police]] estimated that the parade attracted a crowd in excess of 100,000.<ref>{{cite news |first1=Deborah |last1=Linton |first2=John |last2=Scheerhout |title=Blue Monday: Boom for Manchester as 100,000 set to celebrate at City's FA Cup parade |url=http://menmedia.co.uk/manchestereveningnews/news/s/1421442_blue-monday-boom-for-manchester-as-100000-set-to-celebrate-at-citys-fa-cup-parade |work=Manchester Evening News |publisher=MEN Media |date=23 May 2011 |access-date=29 August 2011 |archive-date=28 May 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110528053612/http://menmedia.co.uk/manchestereveningnews/news/s/1421442_blue-monday-boom-for-manchester-as-100000-set-to-celebrate-at-citys-fa-cup-parade |url-status=dead }}</ref>

